===Etymology 1===
From Middle English and Old English upper case letter {{m|ang|E}} and split of {{m|ang|Æ}}, ''Ea'', ''Eo'', and {{m|ang|Œ}}, from five 7th century replacements of Anglo-Saxon Futhorcs by Latin letters:
* [[File:Runic letter ehwaz.png|class=skin-invert-image|15px|Anglo-Saxon Futhorc ᛖ (e)]] Old English letter {{m|ang|E}}, from replacement by Latin letter {{m|ang|E}} of the Anglo-Saxon Futhorc letter {{m|ang|ᛖ}}.
* [[File:Rune-Æsc.png|class=skin-invert-image|15px|Anglo-Saxon Futhorc letter ᚫ (æ)]] Old English letter {{m|ang|Æ}} from replacement by Latin ligature {{m|la|Æ}} of the Anglo-Saxon Futhorc letter {{m|ang|ᚫ}}.
* [[File:Rune-Ear.png|class=skin-invert-image|15px|Anglo-Saxon Futhorc ᛠ (ea)]] Old English digraph ''Ea'', from replacement by Latin digraph {{m|la|EA}} of the Anglo-Saxon Futhorc letter {{m|ang|ᛠ}}.
* [[File:Runic letter iwaz.png|class=skin-invert-image|15px|Anglo-Saxon Futhorc ᛇ (ēo)]] Old English digraph ''Eo'' from replacement by Latin digraph {{m|enm|EO}} of Anglo-Saxon Futhorc {{m|ang|ᛇ}}.
* [[File:Rune-Eðel.png|class=skin-invert-image|15px|Anglo-Saxon Futhorc ᛟ (œ)]] Old English letter {{m|ang|Œ}} from replacement by Latin ligature {{m|la|Œ}} of the Anglo-Saxon Futhorc letter {{m|ang|ᛟ}}.

